  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Processing of chia seed into value added products</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Although chia has been known for centuries in Guatemala, Mexico and south-western parts of the USA, it has recently been rediscovered for commercial consumption in the Health Food markets, thanks to its high levels of Omega 3, antioxidants, protein and fibres. Functional Products S.A. started the production of chia in Argentina, in the year 2000. In 2006, shareholders of Functional Products S.A. established Productos Funcionales DelAgro SRL in Santa Cruz de la Sierra &#x2013; Bolivia, to diversify their sources and reduce vulnerability to climatic conditions. Relying on outgrowers for the supply of raw seeds, Productos Funcionales DelAgro S.R.L. has been using the services of third parties, for cleaning and processing. This is done in plants designed for the handling of soybeans, wheat and corn, but because the chia seeds are much smaller (about 25% the size of sesame seeds) the processing losses are very high (up to 22%). At the same time, processing plants do not give a high priority to the processing of chia, as the volumes are small (compared to their core products), and chia is frequently just processed in between larger soy or corn jobs. This can lead to additional losses, as the chia will spoil if it is not processed soon (1 to 2 days) after the harvest.Functional Products S.A. and Productos Funcionales DelAgro S.R.L., facing a continuous strong growth in demand, now decided to constitute a joint venture to establish a dedicated chia seed processing facility, designed for the small chia seeds, which will substantially reduce the handling losses. At the same time, production of chia seeds will be increased 3-fold, by expanding the cultivation areas, from 1,500 ha to 4,200 ha. This will be achieved by addition of at least 30 new outgrowers, as well as expansion of the cultivated areas with current outgrowers.The dedicated processing facility will be unique and completely new for Bolivia, and will also include the first (value adding) production of chia oil for consumption.</narrative>
  </description>
